---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, packages, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['included', 'What is included'],
  ['install', 'Install a package'],
  ...packages.map(({ id, name }): [string, string] => [id, name]),
  ['maintain', 'Scope and updates'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Build your pi extension setup"
  description="Phosphor is the workspace. pi is the engine. Packages add the capabilities that make the agent useful across your actual workflow."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="included">
    <h2>Coding works first. Extensions take it further.</h2>
    <p>
      Start with a model and pi’s local coding tools. You do not need to install every package to
      edit code. Add capabilities deliberately: research a library, read a ticket, query an
      operational tool, or delegate an independent investigation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phosphor already bundles six extensions: artifacts, context breakdown, MCP status reporting,
      opt-in Headroom integration, malformed tool-name repair, and a wrong-worktree path guard. <strong
        >MCP status reporting is not the MCP adapter.</strong
      > The adapter itself is a separate install.
    </p>
    <p>
      Packages can contain executable extensions, reusable skills, prompt templates, and themes. A
      skill describes a procedure; an extension can register tools or a provider. <a
        href={doc('extensions.md#bundled-extensions-phosphors-own')}>Bundled extension reference</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>Install from Settings</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Extensions</strong>.</li><li>
        Review the Recommended catalog and the package’s source. Choose the capability you need,
        then <strong>Add</strong>.
      </li><li>Watch the job output and confirm the package appears in Installed.</li><li>
        For a package outside the catalog, use Add by spec with an npm, Git, or local-path spec.
        Choose global or workspace scope.
      </li><li>
        Start a new session so pi loads the package. Settings for Claude Code, Web access, and
        Computer use appear as nested tabs when their packages are present.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-extensions')}
      alt="Phosphor Extensions settings displaying recommended and installed packages with example data."
      caption="Install the capability, then configure it in its own tab"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <aside class="notice">
      <strong>Install is a trust decision</strong><p>
        Packages run code with full system access. The catalog is a starting point, not a sandbox or
        a guarantee about future versions. Review source and requirements before installing. Tools
        may use external services, require keys, or incur charges.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  {
    packages.map(({ id, name, benefit, spec, url }) => (
      <section id={id}>
        <>
          <h2>{name}</h2>
          <p>{benefit}</p>
          <pre tabindex="0" aria-label={`Install ${name}`}>
            <code>pi install {spec}</code>
          </pre>
        </>
        {id === 'claude' && (
          <p>
            Requires the official Claude Code CLI and an authorized account. Follow the{' '}
            <a href="/guides/claude-code/">Claude setup and multiple-account tutorial</a>, including
            its end-to-end provider check.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'mcp' && (
          <p>
            Required for Phosphor’s connected-service tools. Installing it does not grant service
            access. Next,{' '}
            <a href="/guides/connectors/">add a connector, authorize it, and test it</a>.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'web' && (
          <p>
            Open the Web access tab after installation and configure the search provider you intend
            to use. Requirements vary by provider. Verify a small search and URL fetch in a new
            session; request source links and inspect the tool results.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'subagents' && (
          <p>
            There is no separate settings tab. Start a new session and use the package’s delegation
            tools for bounded, independent work. Additional model calls consume tokens and quota;
            delegation is not free parallelism.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'computer' && (
          <p>
            Read the Computer use tab and the package’s platform requirements. Grant the required OS
            accessibility permissions explicitly. Verify observation of a test window before
            allowing clicks or typing in sensitive applications.
          </p>
        )}
        <p>
          <a href={url}>Package documentation and source ↗</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    ))
  }
  <section id="maintain">
    <h2>Scope, updates, and verification</h2>
    <p>
      Global installs apply across workspaces. A project install uses <code
        >pi install -l npm:package-name</code
      > from that project and writes project configuration. pi loads packages according to its scope and
      project-trust rules; a declared package is not proof that a running session has loaded it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use Update all in Extensions, or update a specific package by spec. On current pi, <code
        >pi update --extensions</code
      > updates packages, while bare <code>pi update</code> updates pi itself. A pinned package may need
      an explicit version change.
    </p>
    <p>
      After any package change, start a new session. Give the new capability a small test and
      inspect the actual tool output. Installing a package, authenticating a service, and completing
      a successful model-driven call are three different checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md"
        >pi package installation and scope</a
      > · <a href="https://pi.dev/packages">Browse the package ecosystem</a> · <a
        href={doc('extensions.md')}>Phosphor extension contract</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
